<%# The attention band: what needs you, then where the money actually goes.
    Local: `presenter` (HomePresenter). See the UI design spec §4.2.

    ONE KIND OF PROBLEM NOW, WHERE THERE WERE THREE (Task 6). The pool era listed an overdrawn
    ACCOUNT and a pool with no account beside the envelopes whose own status needed attention:

      * The ORPHAN term is gone with the shape. A category belongs to no account and needs none —
        allocating money moves nothing physical (two-ledger spec §2) — so "nothing can fund this"
        has no cause left to describe.
      * The OVERDRAWN ACCOUNT term is gone because the fix beside a row is an ALLOCATION, and an
        allocation cannot touch the physical ledger. Offering one against a real bank overdraft
        would be a mistake proposed to fix a problem it cannot reach — the same ruling that keeps a
        fix off an overdue bill whose category already holds the money. The standing band names the
        account and the figure in red and the accounts band prints the negative balance; only the
        button went away. %>
<% problems = presenter.attention_categories %>
<% rows = presenter.waterfall %>

<%# The cutoff marks where the cash ran out, which is AFTER the last category that received any of
    it: one funded $355 of $400 did get money, and the spec's §4.2 waterfall draws the line beneath
    it.

    ASKED, NOT DECIDED HERE. That rule and its `|| rows.length` fallback used to be written out in
    this line and again in distributions/_waterfall.html.erb — one money-shaped decision with two
    readers, both in ERB. HomePresenter#cutoff carries the gate this band needs (something actually
    short); Waterfall.cutoff carries the rule, for both screens. %>
<% cutoff = presenter.cutoff %>

<section class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded" aria-labelledby="attention-heading">
  <div class="px-6 py-4 border-b border-gray-200">
    <h3 id="attention-heading" class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900">
      <% if problems.any? %>
        <%= pluralize(problems.size, "thing") %> need<%= "s" if problems.one? %> you
      <% elsif presenter.covered? %>
        Nothing needs you
      <% else %>
        <%# Real state, not a corner: every rate category reads `left_to_spend`, so a period can be
            short with nothing flagged. "Nothing needs you" above a gap is the exact failure this
            band exists to prevent. %>
        Nothing is flagged, but you're still short
      <% end %>
    </h3>
  </div>

  <% problems.each do |category| %>
    <% status = presenter.status_for(category) %>
    <% fix = presenter.fix_for(category) %>
    <div class="px-6 py-3 border-b border-gray-100 <%= "bg-status-danger-light" if status.state == :overdrawn %>"
         data-problem-category="<%= category.name %>">
      <div class="flex justify-between items-baseline">
        <span class="font-medium text-gray-900"><%= category.name %></span>
        <span class="text-sm text-status-danger">
          <%# `changed_after_distributing:` through the presenter, never a query of the partial's
              own — same reason as `status` above: the period this compares against is
              `presenter.today`'s, and a band reaching for `Date.current` here would date the
              distribution differently from every other figure on the screen. Silent on every state
              but `:behind` (HomeHelper#pool_status_label owns that gate). %>
          <%= pool_problem_label(status,
                                 changed_after_distributing: presenter.changed_after_distributing?(category)) %>
        </span>
      </div>

      <%# THE FIX (spec §4.2). Every problem gains a specific, clickable answer naming a source that
          can genuinely cover it — or, when nothing can, says so in words rather than offering a
          dead button.

          FOUR BRANCHES, AND EACH IS A DIFFERENT ANSWER RATHER THAN THE SAME ONE SAID FOUR WAYS.
          Three of them decline to offer a button, and they decline for reasons that send the reader
          to three different places, so collapsing them would be the same defect as a silent row:

          1. THE MONEY IS ALREADY THERE. An overdue bill fires on a DATE and a missing payment, not
             on the balance, so a category holding every penny of its premium is still red.
             HoldingStatus#funding_gap is zero, and the honest answer is that the bill needs paying
             rather than funding — the same sentence the distribution screen's alerts band prints
             about the same category, in the same voice.
          2. THE MONEY IS COMING. Same principle one step out: the waterfall below this row funds
             this category's whole ask, so a move today would rob another to solve something the next
             distribution solves by itself. Read off that same waterfall rather than recomputed, so
             the two bands cannot contradict each other — see HomePresenter#covered_by_waterfall?.
          3. NOTHING CAN COVER IT. Every other party was asked, AVAILABLE included; see
             HomeHelper#fix_gap_sentence.
          4. A SOURCE — the button, and its consequence.

          THE FIFTH BRANCH IS DELETED: "no account — assign it to one before any money can reach
          it". `HomePresenter#fix_for` used to answer nil for an orphan pool, and there is no orphan.

          The consequence line is AllocationsHelper's, off ReallocationPresenter::Candidate — the
          same sentence, from the same Data object, that the screen this button opens will print
          half a second later. There is one answer to "what would this move cost", and
          ReallocationPresenter.source_order is likewise the one answer to "from where". %>
      <% if !fix.needs_money? %>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 mt-2">Its money is already there — this needs paying, not funding.</p>
      <% elsif fix.covered? %>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 mt-2">Its money is coming — the next distribution funds this in full.</p>
      <% else %>
        <% fix_path = { to_category_id: category.id, amount: fix.amount_param } %>
        <div class="mt-2">
          <% if fix.source %>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 mb-2">This has to come from money you already have.</p>
            <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2">
              <%= link_to fix_button_label(fix),
                          new_allocation_path(**fix_path, from_category_id: fix.source.id),
                          class: "btn btn-primary" %>
              <%# No `from_category_id`: the reallocation screen takes any subset of the three, so
                  this arrives with the destination and the amount filled in and every source still
                  to choose from — including the ones this band declined to propose. %>
              <%= link_to "Take from somewhere else…",
                          new_allocation_path(**fix_path),
                          class: "btn btn-secondary" %>
            </div>
            <p class="text-sm mt-2 <%= allocation_damage_class(fix.candidate) %>">
              <%= fix.source.name %> <%= allocation_damage_sentence(fix.candidate) %>
            </p>
          <% else %>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600"><%= fix_gap_sentence(fix) %></p>
          <% end %>
        </div>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <%# Whenever there is a gap OR something needs you.

      It used to be the gap alone, and that suppressed the plan on exactly the screen that needed
      it: `won't make it · Aug 18` rendered under "You're covered this period" with no way to see
      that this period's distribution funds the bill in full. HoldingStatus describes the category
      NOW, the waterfall describes the plan, and showing the warning while hiding its own resolution
      is the worst combination of the two.

      Still silent when nothing is short and nothing is flagged, which is principle 3 intact.

      And `rows.any?` first, because "something needs you" is NOT the same question as "is there a
      plan to show": the zero-ask reject can empty the list outright, so this heading could render
      over nothing, and on a money screen an empty headed band reads as data that failed to load. %>
  <% if rows.any? && (!presenter.covered? || problems.any?) %>
    <div class="px-6 py-4 border-t border-gray-200" aria-labelledby="waterfall-heading">
      <%# Sentence case, not the `uppercase` treatment this codebase gives short labels: that renders
          as WHERE YOUR MONEY GOES, which shouts a whole sentence — and it is the literal copy the
          design spec asks for. Present tense, because this band has never described a completed
          distribution: it describes what the NEXT one would do. %>
      <%# THE TWO BANDS ANSWER DIFFERENT QUESTIONS AND MUST SAY SO. The rows above offer
          `Take $553.85` — HoldingStatus#funding_gap, the whole cumulative hole, read from the live
          holding. The rows below print `$35.00 of $171.43` — #required, THIS period's share of it,
          post-sweep. Both figures are right and both are wanted (see HomePresenter#fix_amount_for),
          and on the demo they sit inches apart on one screen. That is the bridge, and it belongs in
          the band's own framing rather than as a seventh clause on every row. %>
      <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-4 mb-2">
        <div>
          <p id="waterfall-heading" class="text-xs font-semibold tracking-wide text-gray-500">Where your money goes</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">
            This period's share — what the next distribution puts in, not the whole gap.
          </p>
        </div>
        <%# THE ACTION THIS BAND IS DESCRIBING, one click away. Home tells a user "the next
            distribution funds this in full" and, until this link existed, gave them no way to reach
            it. `turbo_prefetch: false` because this GET takes write locks — see
            shared/_nav_section.html.erb, where the same link says why at length. %>
        <%= link_to "Distribute this period",
                    new_distribution_path,
                    class: "btn btn-secondary",
                    data: { turbo_prefetch: false } %>
      </div>

      <%# Two groups rather than a flag inside one loop: the cutoff is then written once and renders
          correctly at either edge — before every row when the first category got nothing, and after
          the last row when the money ran out inside it. A nil cutoff (nothing short) leaves one
          group, and no line. %>
      <% groups = cutoff ? [rows.first(cutoff), rows.drop(cutoff)] : [rows] %>
      <% groups.each_with_index do |group, index| %>
        <% if index == 1 %>
          <div class="border-t-2 border-dashed border-status-danger my-2 pt-2 text-sm text-status-danger font-medium">
            — ran out here · <%= number_to_currency(presenter.shortfall) %> unfunded —
          </div>
        <% end %>
        <% group.each do |row| %>
          <div class="flex justify-between text-sm py-1">
            <span class="text-gray-900"><%= row[:category].name %></span>
            <span class="text-gray-600">
              <%= number_to_currency(row[:funded]) %> of <%= number_to_currency(row[:needed]) %>
            </span>
          </div>
        <% end %>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  <% end %>
</section>
